> Is the concern with const type ids that we might not be able to guarantee two types have the same id at both compile time and runtime in a new scheme? Or is it that the current ways type ids can be abused are extra nasty at compile time and want to try fix them first?

The latter, sort of:

There are potential changes we might want to make to the implementation, *not to prevent abuse*, but rather to guarantee `TypeId`s are sound even in the presence of hash collisions, such as relying on the linker to allocate one *address* per type used with `TypeId`, *instead of* relying on hashes.

Those changes would *break* any code that might be abusing `TypeId` at *compile-time*, something that was impossible before #72488, but now is already possible on beta, e.g.: ([playground](https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=beta&mode=debug&edition=2018&gist=a23c4d1d387ca10b33bb083c2d2dc863)):
```rust
use std::any::TypeId;

const TYPE_ID: TypeId = TypeId::of::<u32>();
const TYPE_ID_HASH: u64 = unsafe {
    union Transmute { from: TypeId, to: u64 }
    Transmute { from: TYPE_ID }.to
};

type Array = [u8; (TYPE_ID_HASH as u8) as usize + 123];

pub fn foo(a: &Array) -> &[u8; 188] { a }
```

Normally we wouldn't worry this much about an unstable internal detail, but given there is no easy way to get at the value inside, people *have* and *will* transmute `TypeId`, which we also saw happening with `mem::Discriminant`, IIRC (cc @lcnr).

